[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b5785c-9c86-58fa-8702-7b20eaf94717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540600} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d02e90-1b24-51db-a87e-21fe5d5fa85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540804} {:answer/id #uuid "da8619fc-53ce-586e-8d07-73efa17d3b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540896} {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} {:answer/id #uuid "f746d0ee-4d45-5138-a444-dd8fb7c426b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa476148-4520-535e-8de8-c555998b437f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0b25f6-0e44-56a6-a167-f47173302a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-113672" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{4/5}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815} :answer-field/id #uuid "16743b63-9c0b-507f-81e5-a93679be7ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0beb016-ee67-53e9-a1ae-c396296738c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352258" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-6+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=2$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x(-6+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-6x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-3(3)^{2}+\\frac{5(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4})-(-3(2)^{2}+\\frac{5(2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540930} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b40e7c83-4aaa-5842-b161-090408598820" :question/math-academy-id "q-279714" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(3\\le X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}){\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}){\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5-4} \\\\  & =10{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+5{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1} \\\\  & ≈0.658\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8241bb7f-109e-55f9-be72-8316a22c2b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8241bb7f-109e-55f9-be72-8316a22c2b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540751} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da18180-5a36-5125-8324-5c1b0c6c8774" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b506000a-3cca-5384-98ce-1df2cd5d8561" :question/math-academy-id "q-223671" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(x+7)(2x-5)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=-(x+7)(2x-5),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-7+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{2}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36064afd-4624-50aa-b9f7-869ddad593ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540613} {:answer/id #uuid "3e30535b-824e-5d84-96a9-2fa046e67302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540632} {:answer/id #uuid "576b7268-d013-5459-8484-febaf9a3d59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd73e85-3ada-52db-9939-f4f819c081ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} :answer-field/id #uuid "889a0978-0193-5e72-b84c-66f0c08de47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6915e10-1543-50b0-a358-2c56a1366444" :question/math-academy-id "q-336744" :question/problem "A fermentation tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of minerals enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *minerals* enter the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}(18-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 18\\,\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)-2.2{\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =7.2\\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})-2.2{(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =7.2,\\beta =-2.2,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =7.2-(-2.2)=9.4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540907} :answer-field/id #uuid "66359473-563b-5acf-a793-afdba80eb7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf9b3381-1894-58e7-8430-4bb3204c16fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-279683" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{x}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{0}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{5-1} \\\\  & =1{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{0}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{5}+5{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =0.337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4662ff5c-1779-587c-8818-862f89cec631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540650} {:answer/id #uuid "57b912a8-d2ae-5a4c-a949-6c6b3b4e2b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540676} {:answer/id #uuid "78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540731} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3c24fb-cfb1-5483-9975-5fe28ec66e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540791} {:answer/id #uuid "c78c0d6a-5d3e-5010-92bc-68ad33e022bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540731} :answer-field/id #uuid "e825c5c1-d92a-52fc-8cd7-189d7de3d71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" :question/math-academy-id "q-124785" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca003331d04113119019996f15296ec2ab16fa0c14227a12f5f421c4c4103746.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540541} {:answer/id #uuid "1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(2y+5)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540624} {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109259" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9ad43f4bddaeab8327e5405193471c005962013355ec84c4a6f858640216dd7.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-5y-4$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)=-2y-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)\\sqrt{1+{(-2y-5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{26+20y+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-344647" :question/problem "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\pi /4-(-\\pi /4))}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(-\\sqrt{2})-(\\sqrt{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f220aba-10fa-5140-91ce-bb3c746dbdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4a5e43e-e7a6-55b0-a709-f89ec3a86933" :question/math-academy-id "q-133293" :question/problem "Let $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ be the position vector of the eagle. Find the distance between the eagle and its nest at time $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe eagle is approximately$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$ away from its nest after $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Since the nest is at the origin, the distance between the bird and its nest at time $t=5$ is $|r(5)|.$\n\nWe know that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3}.$ Evaluating the function at $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right),\\frac{95}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{95}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(5)| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{95}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9\\,025}{9}} \\\\  & ≈31.68\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e8b185-9e4d-524a-98a5-45013ad83009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540543} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540544} {:answer/id #uuid "5cae022b-6032-5575-aa99-3439709b9c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540684} {:answer/id #uuid "624013de-ac75-5b7a-a4d8-e9822947abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540694} {:answer/id #uuid "6f15d79f-35df-5dfd-909e-20ba976f3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540544} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7d6654-0ebf-57f1-bbf5-229f64325368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-85233" :question/problem "![](images/75/751d38a1a37afd277cbd54850abf61db898a231b49ad0d4bcaa5964bbab601e1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{4}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c25b5e8-29a2-5d6a-9195-b39381be0aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 6 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541245} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b961-8e87-550d-a8e0-0ee0a3e468f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 t + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541249} {:answer/id #uuid "9a77f260-9187-5326-8c4a-24a29bc84c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541253} {:answer/id #uuid "a0404454-ff13-50be-8ab6-15207e83bcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541254} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a7ebb6-60db-5509-bee6-228908d07bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 - \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c25b5e8-29a2-5d6a-9195-b39381be0aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 6 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541245} :answer-field/id #uuid "907eeef4-8e8b-5f0c-8e61-37d6501dc067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14f8b19c-03b3-520d-8d8f-1445fd24196c" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b6f872-a2b6-51b5-91ce-9e9804ef80bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 4 t e^{t} - 4 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541235} {:answer/id #uuid "46bc6725-4437-5018-af20-90eb8255f285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 t e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541240} {:answer/id #uuid "6102edf7-bb37-51f8-ac13-c1becf20b623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 4 t e^{t} - 4 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{2}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541246} {:answer/id #uuid "74a3f346-66c3-5125-bb1c-f79e2e4599f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 4 t e^{t} + 4 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541247} {:answer/id #uuid "9203d069-6357-50d2-a652-33020f0f9f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{4}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46bc6725-4437-5018-af20-90eb8255f285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 t e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541240} :answer-field/id #uuid "179914be-111c-5b2b-83ab-1e8d35c73d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "530874a1-6640-5e7f-9a0e-6e65871c39a9" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + 2 t - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)=2 t + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 2}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 2 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + 2 t - 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ 2 t \\left(- t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2303f608-48b7-5784-990e-095149d5db5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1 + \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} + 6 e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541238} {:answer/id #uuid "540436d5-d613-53ad-a74e-19e0ecec9b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 - \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541243} {:answer/id #uuid "a4edab06-cf8e-5a76-b2d8-e59ca8e4c993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 12 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541255} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c01684-3333-58d5-b391-8be426accc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541263} {:answer/id #uuid "f696a871-8842-5610-9f5c-2f864cd239ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- t - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 9 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4edab06-cf8e-5a76-b2d8-e59ca8e4c993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 12 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541255} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c801dc-024c-575a-b842-e243d4bc3807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fe06042-8aa0-5170-bdc7-f300c6962914" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 3)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac5cf81-07cf-5d63-a1dd-e7ba4f943e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541237} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3188b0-d884-5377-ab31-f4dae48d4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 2 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541241} {:answer/id #uuid "c20cd946-fa72-5557-a918-b39037346b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 + \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541264} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e8a05b-0454-5d97-ad03-f310df1208a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541267} {:answer/id #uuid "fac3b51a-060c-5220-8c81-494b3f2428c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c3188b0-d884-5377-ab31-f4dae48d4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 2 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541241} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd4fa90-6aec-5417-a617-5719d0fd4b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af315a0d-cdb8-50ea-94f3-8aca5e2aec09" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e01a93-bfdd-5d60-b008-8c0b11015eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} + 2 e^{t} \\\\ -1 + \\frac{3}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541234} {:answer/id #uuid "3d02bfeb-b1f1-5e6c-b4a5-b5137a3b7eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541239} {:answer/id #uuid "56d49eae-7ec5-5872-b426-29429faf867e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} + 5 t e^{t} - 5 e^{t} \\\\ 3 - \\frac{6}{t} + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541244} {:answer/id #uuid "a594b3fd-3990-519f-9b9e-837f8cfe919d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ 2 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 - \\frac{3}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541256} {:answer/id #uuid "cb52a2d0-993c-52aa-a62e-df68c4e7cfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 + \\frac{9}{t} \\\\ 2 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d02bfeb-b1f1-5e6c-b4a5-b5137a3b7eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541239} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a83f6e-4bfe-53c6-99e0-f32bda4ce4df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6931a3c-17b8-55a5-9c5d-652d29b81597" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} - 3 t + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)=2 t - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t - 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t - 3\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\left(2 t - 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t - 3}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} - 3 t + 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - t - 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0caec6d9-3660-5e5d-93f8-273e3177e9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541450} {:answer/id #uuid "37dbe3a0-18a2-5453-a5d4-551f3906a5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541542} {:answer/id #uuid "99a09e67-57a3-595e-8038-9fd0c9ae9d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541742} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bf4cb6-3664-5e42-9eac-88072f5bc8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541836} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5bae98-5138-5fd6-bec2-d20b4cb20409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0caec6d9-3660-5e5d-93f8-273e3177e9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ec3545-9073-58ce-bcc7-3fa4b6d955fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c69e568-3aa6-590d-a547-523c7522fc27" :question/math-academy-id "q-141252" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 1)}{(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/1018201037350f6bd231a384219d03c03fc1e2165a088e158c9ba2b2bfbb1022.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27db9b54-964b-54ac-bbac-bbad8d91280d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541505} {:answer/id #uuid "321aa89f-4cc2-54c3-89b7-8028876aeb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541526} {:answer/id #uuid "78247b87-c63b-59e2-9881-7f7af5d616aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541671} {:answer/id #uuid "86ec3eb1-3f81-55ae-aee0-50eda1b75a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541695} {:answer/id #uuid "beaf8493-9a78-55da-98d2-762d26ef1c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321aa89f-4cc2-54c3-89b7-8028876aeb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541526} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e86af00-a67b-5794-9225-1dd62d11259f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1873fa45-940e-5434-9a04-82891d309ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100180" :question/problem "Calculate the value of ${\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{0}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df6c47b-c813-591d-800c-7da989a956a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "316d8b52-05f5-5660-9987-6f2357a674ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-342789" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa4e6a0-3aeb-559f-96d7-bd92f700b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541445} {:answer/id #uuid "b490045f-fa26-53d0-83e1-8ad88e404827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541793} {:answer/id #uuid "b7410f52-b281-5541-8a42-cabcaa3e3486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541800} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae0bda-2f60-5c1b-bf1e-e3f18a773891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541954} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f995b-cc21-5f36-9851-e5fb6941662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aa4e6a0-3aeb-559f-96d7-bd92f700b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541445} :answer-field/id #uuid "41165c67-5f21-5f55-b678-99fd5befca9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e631ed-e489-57e1-8328-8ad962002fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75382" :question/problem "$3+\\frac{3}{2}x-1-\\frac{7}{4}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+\\frac{3x}{2}-1-\\frac{7x}{4} &= (\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{7x}{4})+(3-1) \\\\ &= (\\frac{6}{4}-\\frac{7}{4})x+(3-1) \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{6-7}{4}\\right)x+2 \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ad0bb0-44b4-5377-b18a-d503eaa1b661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541440} {:answer/id #uuid "2399beaf-eace-5a34-9002-6ad316000d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541496} {:answer/id #uuid "bd150fa0-ce1f-5591-a343-840beb560c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541817} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ccf14d-454f-5062-8149-cc1e7174890e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541942} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8ca8d0-5bd2-5279-a5a0-2e48e66c5361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd150fa0-ce1f-5591-a343-840beb560c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541817} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5f0102-7a6b-5740-bb7e-b8c83d6d91f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b160342-1c9b-5cb6-9f7b-8bf55c5af2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141356" :question/problem "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0}{(2\\cdot 0-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\left(-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\right) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8f39669eaedc42a26d28d96f6dbb961080816e7fadd182b79a260c8de27b3d1a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf16f7e4-17c9-549f-a864-943b15c55740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16f7e4-17c9-549f-a864-943b15c55740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541860} :answer-field/id #uuid "9960e886-6591-52e7-b6de-f3f58994bcca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4234aa-c19a-50c4-8afe-1f256cd2c1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4234aa-c19a-50c4-8afe-1f256cd2c1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541749} :answer-field/id #uuid "c603d277-c436-515e-9401-4e14d4e32737" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bd1b924-fea7-5666-9b66-85d3682d3092" :question/math-academy-id "q-362903" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b2/b2485a1b1386468904947c52ff3f52997f72597da84384ae41d246f386a39232.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7af24af-4c8d-5f43-b558-8db7ccd96709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7af24af-4c8d-5f43-b558-8db7ccd96709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541939} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f4ee068-a4ba-5955-9803-7974762ed968" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "651d48b6-dcdd-5bc2-87b4-a66ffecf04ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-175589" :question/problem "If $\\frac{1}{15}=-\\frac{m}{3},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nThen, we remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{3} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{m}{3}\\right) & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is being divided by $3.$ To isolate $m$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{m}{3} & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{15}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{3m}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}m}{\\cancel{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15787c24-e618-53d4-8a29-3dc9499996c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.804" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541465} {:answer/id #uuid "581302f3-81bc-5a00-aa69-41b710ebfdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541597} {:answer/id #uuid "59eed6a5-45fc-5d51-a620-a7b381132dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541600} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f0c17b-c929-51a0-8a87-56cae3e64293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541908} {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0898c05-a5e3-5618-a8b5-3c67837f18a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b7ee989-a51c-50ad-8da0-87452c898ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8837" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+9 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-9 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)=-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…≈-1.352\\,127\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $-1.352\\,127$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+\\pi =1.789\\,465\\,272\\,…≈1.789\\,465 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+2\\pi =4.931\\,057\\,926\\,…≈4.931\\,058\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.789\\,466\\cdot 4.931\\,058≈8.824\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4f18e3-c2c7-5633-ad51-327c11dda76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541451} {:answer/id #uuid "1742151a-fdad-55b5-83cf-1eac826f3b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541470} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb12aa8-c236-5848-bf31-4b538dca5489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541648} {:answer/id #uuid "b235bce1-5f22-5ec4-b929-1a8d2bf6ab80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541788} {:answer/id #uuid "f97c1141-ae12-5ca0-8969-0f2ba9d5bac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f97c1141-ae12-5ca0-8969-0f2ba9d5bac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541945} :answer-field/id #uuid "2224a384-1d96-5035-96d8-8e3991487107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a9df102-8e4d-542b-ad62-072cc2469e7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146287" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|3}=\\frac{22}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{X|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}+{6}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{9}]-{\\left(\\frac{22}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+45-\\frac{484}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc71f80-e83e-56bd-a43b-28c93a53e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541779} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5c497b-6bdc-534b-b9f6-c27062181e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541826} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b36070-55de-58e5-8e82-45b02bf82b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541834} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e096ad-fe8e-5767-ba62-fa5fd8b382c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541840} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa1dbde-f325-5791-9cf0-8c99ff494b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf5c497b-6bdc-534b-b9f6-c27062181e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541826} :answer-field/id #uuid "099ae849-575f-5a4b-9f92-63b55d7f04a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be4431be-3fe9-5dd2-82ee-71868596c05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103815" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fdcad9-364a-5070-981d-f9c1b4551dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541430} {:answer/id #uuid "61f54b36-6f68-535c-92ab-0bc07509609f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541623} {:answer/id #uuid "89c62352-327c-574f-9565-b27be34bf0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\sqrt{3}x-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541699} {:answer/id #uuid "c725779e-c768-516a-9762-dfbab4f6c814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541843} {:answer/id #uuid "e8050d30-bcad-51d5-8a1a-40058eb95f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8050d30-bcad-51d5-8a1a-40058eb95f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541916} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2a0e8c-7f88-5fbd-86b3-0d022972fd45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7a90aa7-52b9-549e-8c2d-341971525e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-141419" :question/problem "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right))\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =(1-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right))\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =(1-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0b04158944bd15aeea3220d4aa63321dd39c423a4f6646ba59c299fa8ef736.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6726d53-1c49-55d4-a9fc-abfb02d98019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6726d53-1c49-55d4-a9fc-abfb02d98019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541766} :answer-field/id #uuid "da69ddf7-0c95-5610-bb80-afdff6c65e1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae7493b-97fa-5e69-b993-6f9d41cd3185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bae7493b-97fa-5e69-b993-6f9d41cd3185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541809} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb8b6806-7bed-5b6d-a52c-5d124c4f7580" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c83da1a3-053f-5553-bc32-57bdb774a02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-351547" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}} $x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4ee331-238e-59a4-8986-69bac9c080bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541449} {:answer/id #uuid "2198478a-0906-5c0b-a84e-218df9b6d508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541493} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b6e35-f362-5186-9453-fac9e3e6cf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541512} {:answer/id #uuid "579c0c58-6b75-506e-975b-78e02a08fb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541596} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7b075f-829a-5526-980c-c8864059ac56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9ae048b-af0f-521f-b439-497b724959ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-21475" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}≈{75.069}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince ${75.069}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={75.069}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘}=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}≈255.069\\,{…}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $75.069\\cdot 255.069≈19\\,148$\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a44896-e16c-55e0-b031-ed3c4525e491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a44896-e16c-55e0-b031-ed3c4525e491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541466} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b01fc71-15e0-5989-a945-a229e3e4981a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441f08b4-04dd-5e42-838c-9e7b01251fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441f08b4-04dd-5e42-838c-9e7b01251fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541567} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea70e8a0-cc70-507c-a9ef-fbc409c64de9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1dd610f-bcb9-5742-82dd-3d71c2887869" :question/math-academy-id "q-362883" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e7020a-b4c1-5c1e-970a-f3080b2f18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542312} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb7ea70-ab48-5b5f-a35f-b10fa2c57fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542339} {:answer/id #uuid "e07e6d25-a555-5fcd-a7a4-bc176e9d0fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542341} {:answer/id #uuid "e433ebda-2236-542b-93b3-4804ba472bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cce96c-e9ed-58e4-b741-d8a8f993b454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02bf06bd-a114-5709-91cd-398487459c7f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+4\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+4\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(4-2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=4-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(4-2t) \\\\  & =-5t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035fa252-c497-5441-a7a9-995b1b999642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542283} {:answer/id #uuid "1931c9f8-d41a-5ef1-b13b-ee9bd8a5e925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542289} {:answer/id #uuid "940e3279-20b0-5c97-a20b-20bc1ccb29af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542313} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abffee-6393-58b4-acdb-d0f5a1c85331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542331} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbe6447-d7b2-5aa3-832a-868998a34289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca5dad-31ea-55b7-94df-b638c15ad9f2" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-2t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-2t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473e8524-7399-51a9-981f-c0aa4668dd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542297} {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d23fa3-0d57-5274-baa4-eef4412f5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{577}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542333} {:answer/id #uuid "d58a6523-8175-5758-9a7a-5450d554c95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542337} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab0414-6ef1-5f05-b520-6b9c768d227c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-24t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4fa989-2ac8-5a30-824a-30041569e8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b732e53-9da1-5d2f-ab3b-dfb9b5fc9cae" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j)-(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-4t+3)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-4t+3$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-4t+3)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t) \\\\  & =16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20b7f03-488d-57c7-b9f2-d236742a9961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542324} {:answer/id #uuid "b77786f1-adbc-5af1-8f60-843037465c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{7}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542326} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} {:answer/id #uuid "f5361eea-4400-528a-850e-0ba9c82a1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542347} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1096ed-014c-57c2-ab1e-36b976f89bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{277}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e14137-0d58-55a7-bd84-61bca100a01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "621c7226-fc49-5cd6-ab81-d4bf845859cb" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c87f5e-5e1e-533e-a685-95f4e3d70e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542288} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc5e25-69f2-53fc-bfbb-ac186ba68379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542295} {:answer/id #uuid "c42403a1-9e8a-5de5-882b-c347f626e69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542329} {:answer/id #uuid "e3968766-f69b-5abb-9c51-38782ded432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542342} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f19d3c7-bdba-598e-85e9-a9f2767c5565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a221b842-bd46-5723-b036-3a6f86665976" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+\\frac{4}{3}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\cdot \\frac{17}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a45f-3cb5-5c5c-82a7-b35bad7ae911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{5}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542287} {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0ed3c-b3f8-5a81-b935-2f28c7723aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542307} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88aef5-06bc-5eb8-bf58-ee6e0e624524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-12t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542323} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aa4113-237e-5e1a-ac67-eb9f38bf7ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aaa0dc-bb9c-53ef-9689-89144abe766e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2a5ec51-bad1-5473-9954-53b213963bd6" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t-2$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-2)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5582abdc-4614-5e71-964a-2d19582d849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7b5275-0ea4-503c-a862-6c7508804d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542309} {:answer/id #uuid "70d93491-99f0-5bd9-b153-73c0784e2eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542310} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} {:answer/id #uuid "9cad3f1b-7cf7-5209-ab65-767609942832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5407b6a-3e15-5951-adb6-f6a3f6d57b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beef72c2-a71c-5197-903a-c3c1f057b873" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11429afc-455f-5c8a-a4df-3cac6e918fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{325}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542286} {:answer/id #uuid "51f0679e-81ac-586f-9a56-1373b928161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542299} {:answer/id #uuid "59abc310-6605-56a9-9327-a4ec3f9272b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542302} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8df974-825a-516c-b007-9a07e0e2b6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542303} {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a67bdf-986e-5011-ab4a-8e0c1bca5fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c47f3d69-bc6d-5af9-a7d6-c2e7d42c806b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j)-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bb6d25-cb28-57c5-9bd8-8b5a3ea0d8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542296} {:answer/id #uuid "69e479cd-9bf0-5481-afeb-58ec5059536d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542308} {:answer/id #uuid "9492d2db-0d16-5473-bbf2-b5753ebbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542314} {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} {:answer/id #uuid "f65df61c-e6ae-5911-bed9-45d693eec2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fcc1cb-695e-577d-916f-00bdd3ab93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d489cb8a-37ac-5057-8882-4a94eb183baf" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fc2385-2b81-5188-bb65-8be9a6b72cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542290} {:answer/id #uuid "31d095c6-9894-5dcd-a47c-fbff4a3ac7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542293} {:answer/id #uuid "9972107c-2631-5571-a39a-29d7ef026be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{4}{3}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542315} {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee897a-b9ef-5dd3-8d4a-48a525540411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfdb0a89-889d-56b9-a2eb-f7bf05ae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f4de30-da30-5755-a176-314836964c15" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{145}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{4}{3}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ebd6dd6-7259-5120-97a3-66b51aeb5bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542397} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6c862a-55e0-54f9-b7f6-7a2dc48c416c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542401} {:answer/id #uuid "b062a522-6a46-5f79-9299-18183553eb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542403} {:answer/id #uuid "f9945e02-9035-5167-8318-4361e517c387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542428} {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d2c317-5999-5ae3-83ba-55833a642212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d825389-7a3e-5461-af4a-30fb0473dbe9" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=9-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=9-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}-2\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+3\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((1))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(3\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(3\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(3)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}3-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[3\\cdot \\theta+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(6\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=6\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542368} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542387} {:answer/id #uuid "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542391} {:answer/id #uuid "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542399} {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0e834b8-fc6b-56b5-8146-fbd537f8a411" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((4)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}16\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{16}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ddfd73-e817-5e19-bf8d-dd13eb09dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542374} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} {:answer/id #uuid "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542377} {:answer/id #uuid "ae54fd97-3a30-519e-9ebd-e1fb979dbe8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542402} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d4f8e7-781a-5b4c-827e-0040738832b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} :answer-field/id #uuid "b96cd23f-37b9-504c-a679-a848317b445b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5722e3-b420-50b2-8b72-a65be31820d3" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=4$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/05/0505715b5e78700e63d98fc2c1db8e7d4a3074b3e06f4202de1ce5a6831f7571.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((2))^{3})-(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{32}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8160d239-5719-5314-9b34-25360242df8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542577} {:answer/id #uuid "9618b0be-10e0-54af-bddc-144790bcb25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542583} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa66a-4ae8-5eb8-a19d-10e8556f0769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542588} {:answer/id #uuid "c98eda7f-7904-59d5-aed5-f095f55995d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542592} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a59230-cdc0-59cf-8d49-a7d059d179cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2564c8e6-dd58-5512-bb79-d13a805cc112" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x$ along the line $y=2 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(t)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{5} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left((3)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(9-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} {:answer/id #uuid "33be0b46-f243-554c-9d9e-964dc5eb30b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542564} {:answer/id #uuid "6c57458e-2426-55cb-b5d8-65aeae4d3130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542574} {:answer/id #uuid "c56967e6-dc2a-5dcc-9976-37549a83e6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542591} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e63c2b-a619-54eb-9772-8aa7f8b9dbf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d538b1dd-c792-572f-8c1f-8353d9802950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bf9d63c-b3f4-5d38-83c5-c18e8598dd9c" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$ along the line $y=2 x + 1,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t + 1.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t + 1)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(2 t^{2})(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left((2)^{3}-(0)^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left(8-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07329d51-3f5e-5e2e-8441-b2141aff317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35 \\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542558} {:answer/id #uuid "224fc2fd-156e-5950-be40-d81695a7789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542562} {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} {:answer/id #uuid "7105e2b6-33c5-596d-aaf1-e33906b7e9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542575} {:answer/id #uuid "f9544065-9a17-5e0c-a38b-236361697c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9065}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f81595a-0aec-5788-9a31-df107d898cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eade040-7c90-5638-966c-718b41cfb998" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=5 x$ along the line $y=6 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 7.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=6 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,6 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 7\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(6 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(6)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(6)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{37}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{7}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{7}(5 t)(\\sqrt{37})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{7} \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left((7)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left(49-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e40f44d-615f-5865-a602-f53e98d24e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 \\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542561} {:answer/id #uuid "65d7c7ed-cf67-5d74-9d2d-7d230e621f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542572} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc6fca7-a70d-5ad4-b81d-03df2ea33f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542589} {:answer/id #uuid "cc301a85-6415-5f5a-a1ec-c9814b9c754e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9afa8e-4db7-5a3a-a384-3177ceb89729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab95a8c-ac51-5396-919b-6a451ca13ac6" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 x$ along the line $y=4 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=4 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,4 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(4 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(4)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{5}(3 t)(\\sqrt{17})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left((5)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left(25-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b718af0-92d3-59c9-9229-69b2816bd3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542559} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} {:answer/id #uuid "5c916508-666a-5d4f-bc0e-83237918f953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{380}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542569} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd61603-c8b6-5e98-acdd-11a2306975c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542571} {:answer/id #uuid "9a892440-907b-5477-a7e0-478061c8676f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c75bfe-126d-5238-b88a-6cc39af08281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74138378-8762-53b8-ac46-3f0ace177732" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+6+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9920b7-0e21-5c1f-8111-86f228f479e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542580} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2807fa-9092-5089-8280-849eef5d1d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{285}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542581} {:answer/id #uuid "9e06ccd9-0287-543d-9f3b-377e3c903179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(18446744073709551616 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542585} {:answer/id #uuid "bc780127-f10d-5f5e-8853-c61ea96f5041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} :answer-field/id #uuid "927562b5-720d-53ed-bf92-54fcb887de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e94a1c8-32a5-5aeb-82a3-988f3971786f" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({17}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(17 \\sqrt{17}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b716c-5732-5534-84fd-923827a99c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542566} {:answer/id #uuid "6a75de59-ff24-5015-82fd-549b0eabf5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542573} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7a369-2abc-53dd-b2aa-5a817fb5b5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542576} {:answer/id #uuid "871f3101-6660-58fb-8c35-50aad648b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542579} {:answer/id #uuid "8d989555-4656-5801-a461-47d42c1dd876" :answer/type {:db/id