db/id #edb/ref 17592186544014} {:answer/id #uuid "42c48696-2e71-5f50-be40-a4e29348c5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544027} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c46d77-31d5-59c7-bdd0-8d3efac6744d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544045} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1390d-33db-59ee-bdda-27ef9c779d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544050} {:answer/id #uuid "f9644df6-609d-5092-ba27-53c85a68d464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025b96a9-fd3c-5e9b-af50-2eede6f0de28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544014} :answer-field/id #uuid "c113ca18-6b05-5e8f-850c-b3873ed3519f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d41ac9e9-9c5b-5d22-9909-8a38ad72197b" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={150}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c02b906-ae20-50c8-bed5-3bb797391ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c02b906-ae20-50c8-bed5-3bb797391ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544023} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb898007-433e-563f-888f-d36152226b68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba4bbe3-b689-56be-8219-e32d77251473" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406dddda-d7bc-5487-ac5d-0ec92eae17bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406dddda-d7bc-5487-ac5d-0ec92eae17bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544231} :answer-field/id #uuid "978ca5a5-cf10-5fbd-84fe-abe606da7ae7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00bb583b-38c4-5b10-945e-a15928b12671" :question/math-academy-id "q-133228" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the given polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSlope $=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f147060-2e3d-530e-8605-1ba13a4114d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544364} {:answer/id #uuid "9dec9e09-3ab9-51d5-93f5-5553f7a2ae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544429} {:answer/id #uuid "cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544521} {:answer/id #uuid "d87bd39e-276b-5919-ae48-a488a12d6528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544553} {:answer/id #uuid "f4426217-c85d-5fd5-a049-5d1b721018a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544521} :answer-field/id #uuid "479444fd-611e-5a03-bcad-55f19ebf647d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" :question/math-academy-id "q-66801" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${3}^{x}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fd65f66-345e-5e02-a44f-83cf02b3d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd65f66-345e-5e02-a44f-83cf02b3d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544335} :answer-field/id #uuid "248fba5d-72db-5572-a2be-4b009978848e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bcca525-fc41-52cc-be8b-f245ac9162a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133231" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to our polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nThe tangent line is $y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544227} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a47526-4f69-5723-8a3e-3d9029c72ee7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99" :question/math-academy-id "q-319490" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=5+12\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69993dcc-284a-5552-b290-2efb22ae5263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544314} {:answer/id #uuid "92eaaa2e-7d8f-5f56-b19d-d506b27a8024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544409} {:answer/id #uuid "9c197951-1073-5f18-984d-9df842948758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544424} {:answer/id #uuid "b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544463} {:answer/id #uuid "d69a3208-7d65-5d95-b5a7-de518fff3019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544463} :answer-field/id #uuid "265d95ea-7f8d-5e15-8458-659dfbb603bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9880eb88-2023-5ce5-bda9-0c1fc8e76faf" :question/math-academy-id "q-74660" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=4+4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fe/fe0207214fb16ac0871e15f3be7c9fa33670a7df3617f5f9e0b92b45a09c76af.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3965b942-4bfa-58af-87a2-5bee1c709bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\cos^3(2\\theta)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3965b942-4bfa-58af-87a2-5bee1c709bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\cos^3(2\\theta)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544211} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75a3698-1ee7-50e3-b39c-ded471e1e2de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dac1de0-3399-5e2c-82ff-248d3a51fed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-133230" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for our polar curve.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
